# {{ if .DisplayName }}{{ .DisplayName }}{{ else }}{{ .Container }}{{ end }}

The `{{ .Container }}` static site of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}, in project {{ .Project }}: a page in
TypeScript, built with Vite, served from a private S3 bucket through a
CloudFront distribution, with its infrastructure as a CDK app. Scaffolded by
C4 Factory.

## Develop

Open the repository in its devcontainer, which has Node 24 and the Go toolchain.

```sh
npm ci           # install the exact packages in package-lock.json
npm run dev      # serve the page with hot reload on http://localhost:5173
npm test         # unit tests and the stack's assertions, with vitest
npm run build    # the site in dist/, as a deploy copies it
npm run synth    # build the site and write the development templates into cdk.out/
{{- if .Standard }}
make check       # what CI runs: format, lint, type check, test, synth, cfn-lint
make help        # every target
{{- end }}
```

Nothing here deploys or needs an AWS account: synth runs offline, builds the
site with the installed Vite, and needs neither Docker nor credentials.

## Tiers and operations

The stack is built for a tier, `production` or `development`, which is the
place it runs, not the code: `make synth` writes both, into
`cdk.out/production/` and `cdk.out/development/`, and a deploy picks one
(`cdk deploy -c tier=production`; without `-c tier` it is `development`).
The production stack is `{{ .Container }}`, the development one
`{{ .Container }}-development`, so a deploy that names no tier never changes
production. Both are in us-east-1, the only region where CloudFront takes a
web application firewall and a certificate; the site is served from
CloudFront's edge locations wherever its visitors are.

Production keeps the site's bucket when the stack goes, versions it and
keeps each file's earlier versions for 35 days (or the backups' retention,
when longer), puts a web application firewall in front of the distribution,
protects the stack from deletion, traces the deploy's copy with X-Ray and
keeps logs a year; development deletes the bucket and its files with the
stack and keeps logs a month. Both tiers log every request, alarm and have
a dashboard. There is no AWS Backup plan: the site's source is in git.

What the app promises whatever the tier, the recovery objectives and who the
alarms tell, is in `infra/operations.ts`, which C4 Factory renders from the
Container's `spec.operations`: change the block, and the file follows it.

A deploy sets the stack's parameters per environment:

| Parameter        | Default                       | What it is                                                        |
| ---------------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `DomainName`     | empty: CloudFront's own       | the domain the site answers on, such as `www.example.com`         |
| `CertificateArn` | empty                         | an ACM certificate in us-east-1 for `DomainName`, which needs one |
| `AlertEmail`     | `infra/operations.ts`'s email | the address the alarms notify; empty for none                     |

The stack outputs `EntryPoint`, the site's URL, and `AlarmTopicArn`, the
topic its alarms notify. Pointing the domain's DNS record at the
distribution is done where the domain is hosted.

Deploying (`cdk deploy`, after `cdk bootstrap` of the account in us-east-1)
is for its owner, with their own credentials. Each deploy builds the site,
copies it into the bucket beside earlier builds' files, so a page a browser
cached still finds its assets, and clears CloudFront's cache.

## Layout

- `index.html` is the page Vite serves and builds; its `<title>` is the
  site's name, which the page shows as its heading.
- `src/main.ts` finds the page's elements and wires them to the logic;
  `src/greeting.ts` is the logic, free of the DOM, tested by
  `src/greeting.test.ts` in plain Node. `src/index.css` styles the page.
- `public/` is copied into the site as is (the icon).
- `infra/stacks.ts` defines the stack for a tier; `infra/site_stack.ts` is
  the stack: the buckets, the distribution, the firewall, the deploy of the
  built site and its alarms (`infra/alarms.ts`). The stack is named after
  the package. `infra/operations.ts` holds what the Container promises.
{{- if .Standard }}
- `infra/main.ts` is the CDK app `cdk synth` runs (`cdk.json`), and
  `infra/standard.ts` says what each tier means, tags every stack and adds
  CDK Nag's checks. `docs/runbook.md` says how to bring the site's files
  back, roll a deployment back, and what to do when an alarm fires.

Read `AGENTS.md` before changing anything: it is the rulebook, for people and agents.
{{- end }}
